{{also|славен}}
==Serbo-Croatian==

===Alternative forms===
* {{qualifier|now chiefly Serbian|Ekavian}} {{l|sh|Сло̀ве̄н}}
* {{qualifier|now chiefly Serbian|Ijekavian}} {{l|sh|Сло̀вје̄н}}
* {{qualifier|archaic|Ikavian}} {{l|sh|Сло̀вӣн}}

===Etymology===
Ultimately from {{der|sh|sla-pro|*slověninъ}}, but, unlike the alternative forms, not directly inherited. The -a- vowel in the first syllable apparently derives from Russian Church Slavonic or {{der|sh|ru|славяни́н}}, perhaps with influence from {{m|sh|сла̏ва}} by [[folk etymology]].<ref>{{R:sh:Skok1971}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{sh-IPA|Slàvēn}}
* {{hyphenation|sh|Сла|вен}}

===Proper noun===
{{sh-proper noun|g=m|head=Сла̀ве̄н}}

# {{lb|sh|now|chiefly|Bosnia|Croatia}} [[Slav]]
# {{lb|sh|Serbia}} a man from [[Slavonia]]
# {{given name|sh|male}}

====Usage notes====
Until the 18th century, the form of this word with -o- in the first syllable was almost universal throughout the Serbo-Croatian-speaking area (with varying reflexes of [[yat]]). The form with -a- seems to have originated in {{w|Slavonic-Serbian}} and spread under the influence of [[Pan-Slavism]] during the 19th century. By the 20th century, the form with -a- became the most common form in Croatia and all but disappeared from Serbia, effectively reversing the early 19th-century distribution of the two forms.

